# Shell scripts deployed to the Linux server must keep LF endings — a CRLF
# shebang makes the kernel look for an interpreter named "/bin/sh\r", which
# fails at runtime with "required file not found".
*.sh text eol=lf
crontab.example text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf
docker-compose.yaml text eol=lf
dockerfile text eol=lf

# tests/test_version.py parses this file with a regex anchored on `$` in MULTILINE mode, which
# does not match before `\r` — a CRLF working-tree copy (the Windows default without this rule)
# makes the guard test silently find zero version headings instead of comparing versions.
CHANGELOG.md text eol=lf

# PDF is not line-oriented — without this, git's default text-file heuristics (core.autocrlf on
# Windows) can rewrite bare LF bytes inside the binary content to CRLF on checkout, corrupting
# the file's byte offsets (xref table, /Length stream lengths) that pypdf depends on to parse it
# at all. Caught before it shipped: `git check-attr` showed sample.pdf/blank.pdf
# (tests/fixtures/sources/, tests/test_source_extract.py) as "text: unspecified", and `git diff
# --stat` was already diffing them line-by-line like a text file.
*.pdf binary
